WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
Input validation is one of the most important security boundaries in a REST API.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:14.600
Every request body, query string, path value, header, and content type should be treated as untrusted until the application has checked it.

3
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:20.860
TypeScript can describe what the application wants, but runtime validation confirms what the caller actually sent.

4
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.700
A request contract should be specific.

5
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:32.680
Prefer allowlisted schemas with required fields, accepted formats, bounded sizes, permitted enum values, and clear handling for optional data.

6
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:41.580
Validation should reject unexpected structures rather than quietly accepting unknown fields that later code may interpret differently.

7
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.920
This makes route behavior easier to reason about and easier to test.

8
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:54.280
Validation should happen close to the API boundary, before business logic and before authorization decisions that depend on request data.

9
00:00:54.380 --> 00:01:03.580
If a route uses a path value as a resource identifier, or a body value as a requested action, the application should parse and normalize that value first.

10
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.720
Authorization checks should operate on the same meaning the business logic will use.

11
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.200
Safe rejection is part of the contract.

12
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:18.140
Malformed input should receive consistent, helpful, non-sensitive error responses.

13
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.360
Logging should support investigation without storing secrets or full sensitive payloads.

14
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:32.420
A well-designed validation layer turns unpredictable client input into a small set of known shapes that the rest of the TypeScript application can handle confidently.

15
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:38.300
Validation rules should be maintained with the API, not scattered as informal assumptions.

16
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:46.240
When schemas, OpenAPI descriptions, tests, and TypeScript types agree, client teams and server teams have a shared contract.

17
00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:51.220
When they drift, rejected requests and hidden exposure become more likely.
